     "type": "intro",
     "title": "Could You Teach a Tyrant and Stay Good?",
     "content": "Imagine being hired to tutor a teenager who would become one of history's most infamous emperors. You watch him kill his mother. You write his speeches anyway. You grow obscenely rich, then beg to retire. Finally he orders you to open your own veins.\n\nThat was **Lucius Annaeus Seneca** — Stoic philosopher, playwright, billionaire, advisor to **Nero**. The same man who wrote 124 letters urging his friend **Lucilius** to renounce wealth and prepare for death. Hypocrite? Or the most honest moral thinker who ever lived?\n\nBefore we read a single letter, we have to meet the man. Because the **Epistulae Morales ad Lucilium** is not abstract philosophy — it is a confession written by a man trying, in his last years, to become the philosopher he had always claimed to be.",
