     "type": "concept",
     "title": "A Life in Three Acts",
     "content": "Seneca's life splits neatly into three movements, each shaping the **Letters**.\n\n**Act I — Rise (c. 4 BCE - 41 CE)**: Born in **Cordoba, Spain** to a wealthy equestrian family. Trained in Rome in rhetoric and philosophy. Became a senator and respected orator under emperor **Caligula**, who reportedly tried to have him killed for being a better speaker.\n\n**Act II — Exile and Return (41-54 CE)**: Banished by emperor **Claudius** to the island of **Corsica** on a charge of adultery with a princess — possibly a political frame. Spent 8 years writing consolations and tragedies. Recalled by **Agrippina** in 49 CE to tutor her 12-year-old son: **Nero**.\n\n**Act III — Power and Withdrawal (54-65 CE)**: When Nero became emperor at 16, Seneca and the prefect **Burrus** effectively ran Rome for 5 stable years — the **Quinquennium Neronis**. Then Nero turned. Seneca tried to retire in 62 CE. He spent his final 3 years writing the **Letters to Lucilius**. In 65 CE, accused of conspiracy, he was ordered to commit suicide.",
