     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Tacitus's Account: The Long Dying",
     "content": "According to Tacitus's Annals, Seneca received the soldiers calmly, comforted his grieving household, and asked for writing tablets to draft his will, which the centurion refused him, at which point he turned to his assembled friends and remarked that since he could not repay them in property he would leave them the one thing still his to give: the pattern of his own life, if they chose to remember it. He then opened the veins in his arms, but old age and a thin, spare body slowed the bleeding badly, so he also had veins opened in his legs and behind his knees. Still lingering, he asked for and drank hemlock, the same poison associated with Socrates's famous death, but it too failed to act quickly on his weakened frame. Throughout this prolonged process he continued dictating reflections to secretaries, apparently unwilling to let the occasion pass without turning it into a final act of teaching. His wife Paulina attempted to die alongside him by opening her own veins, but soldiers acting on Nero's separate orders intervened and saved her, over Seneca's protest. Finally, still not dead, Seneca was carried into a heated bath, where the steam completed what the blood loss and poison had not, and he suffocated. Tacitus's account is heavy with deliberate echoes of Socrates's death in Plato's dialogues, and many readers have concluded that Seneca, and quite possibly Tacitus in the telling, intended exactly that comparison to be drawn.",
